  "prompt_text": "This schematic illustrates several ways vaccine or drug particles can reach dendritic cells (DCs) in skin and lymphatic tissue. The central idea is that particle size and delivery method determine whether materials stay near the injection site, diffuse into lymphatic vessels, or are carried by cells to lymph nodes.\n\nSmall particles, shown as gray dots, appear to diffuse through tissue and enter initial lymphatics, enabling diffusion-based DC targeting. Hydrogel materials are depicted as a local depot that provides sustained release to skin-resident DCs and nearby lymphatics over time. On the right, microneedles deliver particles directly across the skin barrier to resident DCs.\n\nLarger microparticles seem less able to drain freely and are instead trafficked by cells toward lymph nodes. Nanoparticles are shown interacting with DC surface markers, suggesting both passive and active targeting. Antigen is also present within lymphatic vessels and tissue, indicating uptake during transport.\n\nAt the lower right, the lymph node is shown as a key destination where lymph node–resident DCs capture incoming nanoparticles. Overall, the figure compares complementary delivery strategies for engaging DCs locally in skin and remotely in lymph nodes to shape immune responses.",
